{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"unworldly-narrow-iggypops","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\nThe multi-project model changes things a reader currently learns from the docs as single-project\nfacts: `docs/jira-sync-setup.md:74` presents `project` as one config line and has no section on\nmultiple projects, `docs/config.md:216-260` documents the `[jira]` block as a single scalar project,\nand `docs/migrations.md` describes the ensure registry without the two units this epic adds. A user\nupgrading a client store also needs to know what the new `required_capabilities` entry means for\nolder binaries in their fleet. None of that is documented by the implementation stories, which own\ncode and tests only.\n\n## Approach\n\nDocument the shipped behaviour and record the architectural decision. The ADR is warranted rather\nthan decorative because two decisions here are load-bearing, non-obvious, and expensive to reverse:\nthat the project set is a property of the **store** rather than of a repo, and that the legacy\nmigration writes **no ticket events**. Both were chosen against plausible alternatives — per-repo\nproject lists and an EDIT-per-ticket backfill — and a future reader who does not know why will be\ntempted back toward them.\n\nThe ADR is `docs/adr/0062-*` — the next number after `0061-measurement-provenance-in-attested-evidence.md`.\nIt records the shared-`prev_snapshot` reasoning for store ownership, the short-lock-budget reasoning\nfor the event-free migration, and the conditional-stamp reasoning for compatibility. It also notes the\nrelationship to ADR 0060, whose churn constraint the mapping satisfies by construction.\n\nDocumentation updates cover the mapping and its `bridge projects` verb, the tri-state project field,\nthe repos field, the two ensure units, and the upgrade note for fleets running mixed binary versions.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `docs/adr/0062-many-to-many-tracker-projects.md` (new)\n- `docs/jira-sync-setup.md`\n- `docs/config.md`\n- `docs/migrations.md`\n- `docs/user-guide.md`\n- `docs/ticket-model.md`\n\n## Testing\n\nNo `## Testing` section beyond this note: this story adds no testable behaviour — it is\ndocumentation and an ADR, which `rebar explain plan` names as a legitimately test-free change class.\nVerification is by the repository's existing documentation gates rather than by new tests, and the\nacceptance criteria below name the command that checks each claim.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] `docs/adr/0062-many-to-many-tracker-projects.md` exists and records the store-ownership,\n      event-free-migration, and conditional-stamp decisions with their rejected alternatives; checked\n      by reading the file and confirming each of the three decisions names what it was chosen over.\n- [ ] The Jira sync and config docs describe the project mapping and no longer present a single\n      scalar project as the only option; checked by confirming no remaining passage states the\n      single-project constraint as current behaviour.\n- [ ] `docs/migrations.md` lists both new ensure units alongside the existing built-ins; checked by\n      confirming each registered id appears in the doc.\n- [ ] The docs state what the `multi-project-bridge` capability means for an older binary and what an\n      operator does about it; checked by confirming the upgrade note names both the fail-closed\n      behaviour and the remedy.\n- [ ] The generated CLI reference includes `bridge projects`; checked by running the repository's\n      documentation generation and confirming the verb appears without a manual edit.","id":"7032-d9f3-ed89-4f7b","parent_id":"0e68-41eb-5782-4336","priority":1,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Document the multi-project model and record ADR 0062"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786160111091218001,"uuid":"47e054f5-0528-4d4f-ba20-37b252701470"}